We have 9 people. We want to split them into 3 committees of 3 people each.
First, we choose 3 people out of 9 to form the first committee. The number of ways to do this is $\binom{9}{3} = \frac{9!}{3!6!} = \frac{9 \times 8 \times 7}{3 \times 2 \times 1} = 3 \times 4 \times 7 = 84$.
Next, we choose 3 people out of the remaining 6 to form the second committee. The number of ways to do this is $\binom{6}{3} = \frac{6!}{3!3!} = \frac{6 \times 5 \times 4}{3 \times 2 \times 1} = 20$.
Finally, the remaining 3 people form the third committee. The number of ways to do this is $\binom{3}{3} = 1$.
So the total number of ways to form the committees is $\binom{9}{3} \times \binom{6}{3} \times \binom{3}{3} = 84 \times 20 \times 1 = 1680$.
However, since the committees are identical, we have overcounted the number of ways. Since there are $3! = 6$ ways to arrange the 3 committees, we must divide by $3! = 6$.
So the number of ways to split the 9 people into 3 identical committees of 3 is $\frac{1680}{6} = 280$.
Alternatively, we can compute the multinomial coefficient
$$ \frac{\binom{9}{3}\binom{6}{3}\binom{3}{3}}{3!} = \frac{\frac{9!}{3!6!} \frac{6!}{3!3!} \frac{3!}{3!0!}}{3!} = \frac{\frac{9!}{3!3!3!}}{3!} = \frac{9!}{3!3!3!3!} = \frac{9 \cdot 8 \cdot 7 \cdot 6 \cdot 5 \cdot 4 \cdot 3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1}{(6)(6)(6)(6)} = \frac{362880}{1296} = 280. $$
The answer is 280.

